{"title":"Nuclear Fission","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><div id=\"toc\" class=\"toc\" role=\"navigation\" aria-labelledby=\"mw-toc-heading\"><input type=\"checkbox\" role=\"button\" id=\"toctogglecheckbox\" class=\"toctogglecheckbox\" style=\"display:none\" /><div class=\"toctitle\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2 id=\"mw-toc-heading\">Contents</h2><span class=\"toctogglespan\"><label class=\"toctogglelabel\" for=\"toctogglecheckbox\"></label></span></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-1\"><a href=\"#Nuclear_Fission\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Nuclear Fission</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-2\"><a href=\"#The_Fission_Process\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">The Fission Process</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-3\"><a href=\"#Energy_from_Fission\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Energy from Fission</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-4\"><a href=\"#Chain_Reactions\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">4</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Chain Reactions</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-5\"><a href=\"#Critical_Mass\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">5</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Critical Mass</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-6\"><a href=\"#Fission_Products_and_Radioactivity\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">6</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Fission Products and Radioactivity</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-7\"><a href=\"#Nuclear_Reactors\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">7</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Nuclear Reactors</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-8\"><a href=\"#Nuclear_Weapons\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">8</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Nuclear Weapons</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-9\"><a href=\"#Fusion_vs._Fission\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">9</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Fusion vs. Fission</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-10\"><a href=\"#Example_Calculation\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">10</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Example Calculation</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-11\"><a href=\"#Safety_and_Waste_Disposal\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">11</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Safety and Waste Disposal</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-12\"><a href=\"#See_Also\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">12</span> <span class=\"toctext\">See Also</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Nuclear_Fission\">Nuclear Fission</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Nuclear_Fission&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Nuclear Fission\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><b>Nuclear fission</b> is a nuclear reaction in which a heavy atomic nucleus splits into two (or more) lighter nuclei, along with the release of a large amount of energy, neutrons, and gamma radiation. Fission is the principle behind nuclear reactors and atomic bombs.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"The_Fission_Process\">The Fission Process</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Nuclear_Fission&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: The Fission Process\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>A common fission reaction involves uranium-235 (<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/0/8/d0845f570f52dd8f102f8d8bec3afe48a69a26fd.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"${}^{235}_{92}U$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"37\" height=\"21\" />) absorbing a neutron (<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" />) and splitting:\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/a/0/2a03797c3979c8b5c2ccc036e929d87fff5752c9.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[{}^{235}_{92}U + n \\rightarrow {}^{141}_{56}Ba + {}^{92}_{36}Kr + 3n + \\text{Energy}\\]\" width=\"318\" height=\"20\" />\n</p><p>This means:\n</p>\n<ul><li>A uranium nucleus captures a neutron,</li>\n<li>It becomes unstable and splits into barium-141, krypton-92, and releases 3 neutrons,</li>\n<li>A large amount of energy (approximately 200 MeV) is released.</li></ul>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Energy_from_Fission\">Energy from Fission</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Nuclear_Fission&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: Energy from Fission\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>The energy released comes from the difference in binding energy before and after the fission reaction. Using Einstein’s mass-energy equivalence:\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/2/d/a2db081a1bf14c51df344c557db31c65d0120509.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[E = \\Delta m \\cdot c^2,\\]\" width=\"101\" height=\"19\" />\n</p><p>where <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/c/b/bcb9e62fe9dddb88a478c06a77ee0418140f94e3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\Delta m$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"33\" height=\"14\" /> is the mass defect (difference between mass of original nucleus plus neutron and total mass of fission products).\n</p><p>Typical energy release per fission is about 200 MeV, which is about <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/0/e/80e0847fabc32d80a06a4eb901de7a4e4cbdb276.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3.2 \\times 10^{-11}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"90\" height=\"18\" /> joules.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Chain_Reactions\">Chain Reactions</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Nuclear_Fission&amp;action=edit&amp;section=4\" title=\"Edit section: Chain Reactions\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>The 3 neutrons released can induce fission in other uranium nuclei, creating a <b>chain reaction</b>.\n</p><p>For a sustained chain reaction:\n</p>\n<ul><li>The <b>multiplication factor</b> <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/3/8c325612684d41304b9751c175df7bcc0f61f64f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"14\" /> must satisfy <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/d/f/8df9cf1d6f6e2474e61796d7425ac78ce112893b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k \\geq 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"42\" height=\"15\" />,</li>\n<li><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/0/f/00fab47c0b9d0ce398d03e57d6720129d53434f2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k = 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"42\" height=\"13\" /> means critical (steady state),</li>\n<li><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/1/411e8b2b30fbb26e55ed0d28303d10fa81aa5606.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k &gt; 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"42\" height=\"13\" /> means supercritical (reaction grows exponentially),</li>\n<li><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/2/5/625a97f3f066061fb885933614770f590f3a5f8f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k &lt; 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"42\" height=\"13\" /> means subcritical (reaction dies out).</li></ul>\n<p>Controlling <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/3/8c325612684d41304b9751c175df7bcc0f61f64f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"14\" /> is essential in nuclear reactors.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Critical_Mass\">Critical Mass</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Nuclear_Fission&amp;action=edit&amp;section=5\" title=\"Edit section: Critical Mass\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>The minimum amount of fissile material required to sustain a chain reaction is called the <b>critical mass</b>. It depends on:\n</p>\n<ul><li>Material type (e.g., <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/7/c/77c2903ae65ae24283c89363c5a635149ee50d3f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"${}^{235}U$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"37\" height=\"17\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/f/e/7feeac4795e16f88cc3a42eb8c5dafb7ff3c0dab.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"${}^{239}Pu$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"47\" height=\"17\" />),</li>\n<li>Shape and density,</li>\n<li>Presence of neutron reflectors.</li></ul>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Fission_Products_and_Radioactivity\">Fission Products and Radioactivity</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Nuclear_Fission&amp;action=edit&amp;section=6\" title=\"Edit section: Fission Products and Radioactivity\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Fission fragments are highly radioactive isotopes, causing:\n</p>\n<ul><li>Radioactive decay chains,</li>\n<li>Release of beta and gamma radiation,</li>\n<li>Nuclear waste challenges.</li></ul>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Nuclear_Reactors\">Nuclear Reactors</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Nuclear_Fission&amp;action=edit&amp;section=7\" title=\"Edit section: Nuclear Reactors\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Reactors control the fission chain reaction to produce heat:\n</p>\n<ul><li>Neutron moderators slow down neutrons to increase fission probability,</li>\n<li>Control rods absorb neutrons to regulate <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/3/8c325612684d41304b9751c175df7bcc0f61f64f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"14\" />,</li>\n<li>Heat generated turns water into steam to drive turbines.</li></ul>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Nuclear_Weapons\">Nuclear Weapons</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Nuclear_Fission&amp;action=edit&amp;section=8\" title=\"Edit section: Nuclear Weapons\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Atomic bombs use uncontrolled chain reactions with:\n</p>\n<ul><li>Rapid assembly of a supercritical mass,</li>\n<li>Explosion energy on the order of kilotons to megatons of TNT equivalent.</li></ul>\n<p>Example reaction for bomb-grade uranium:\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/e/c/7eca38305f3e819b0bbb5334d039a7e29105b325.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[{}^{235}_{92}U + n \\rightarrow \\text{Fission fragments} + 2-3n + 200\\ \\text{MeV}\\]\" width=\"387\" height=\"20\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Fusion_vs._Fission\">Fusion vs. Fission</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Nuclear_Fission&amp;action=edit&amp;section=9\" title=\"Edit section: Fusion vs. Fission\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<table class=\"wikitable\" style=\"text-align:center\">\n\n<tbody><tr>\n<th>Aspect</th>\n<th>Fusion</th>\n<th>Fission\n</th></tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Fuel</td>\n<td>Light elements (e.g. H, He)</td>\n<td>Heavy elements (e.g. U-235, Pu-239)\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Waste</td>\n<td>Minimal</td>\n<td>Long-lived radioactive byproducts\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Energy per kg</td>\n<td>Higher than fission</td>\n<td>Lower than fusion\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Conditions needed</td>\n<td>Extremely high temperature and pressure</td>\n<td>Neutron flux and critical mass\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Natural occurrence</td>\n<td>Stars (like the Sun)</td>\n<td>None (rare natural decays only)\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Example reaction</td>\n<td><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/4/5/9458e8f5a6895fe8ecf59c32499556a3335d5997.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"${}^2_1\\text{H} + {}^3_1\\text{H} \\rightarrow {}^4_2\\text{He} + n + 17.6\\ \\text{MeV}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"248\" height=\"21\" /></td>\n<td><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/0/c70b1139dadb888b02fe7303fdbe85a6ce1a9ba3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"${}^{235}_{92}\\text{U} + n \\rightarrow {}^{141}_{56}\\text{Ba} + {}^{92}_{36}\\text{Kr} + 3n + 200\\ \\text{MeV}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"325\" height=\"21\" />\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Applications</td>\n<td>Stars, experimental reactors, hydrogen bombs</td>\n<td>Nuclear power plants, atomic bombs\n</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Example_Calculation\">Example Calculation</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Nuclear_Fission&amp;action=edit&amp;section=10\" title=\"Edit section: Example Calculation\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>If 1 kg of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/7/c/77c2903ae65ae24283c89363c5a635149ee50d3f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"${}^{235}U$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"37\" height=\"17\" /> undergoes complete fission:\n</p>\n<ul><li>Number of nuclei in 1 kg:</li></ul>\n<p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/d/5/ad59faa56a41f9ba24f1d237093158c51f32e7a3.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[N = \\frac{1000\\ \\text{g}}{235\\ \\text{g/mol}} \\times 6.022 \\times 10^{23} \\approx 2.56 \\times 10^{24}\\]\" width=\"348\" height=\"41\" />\n</p>\n<ul><li>Total energy released:</li></ul>\n<p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/d/6/cd616c632caac514e0a2f6608fe3974b0cd2b243.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[E = N \\times 200\\ \\text{MeV} \\times 1.6 \\times 10^{-13}\\ \\text{J/MeV} \\approx 8.2 \\times 10^{13}\\ \\text{J}\\]\" width=\"428\" height=\"19\" />\n</p><p>This equals about 20 kilotons of TNT (the energy released by the bomb dropped on Hiroshima).\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Safety_and_Waste_Disposal\">Safety and Waste Disposal</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Nuclear_Fission&amp;action=edit&amp;section=11\" title=\"Edit section: Safety and Waste Disposal\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Nuclear fission produces radioactive waste requiring:\n</p>\n<ul><li>Shielding and cooling,</li>\n<li>Long-term storage,</li>\n<li>Potential reprocessing and recycling.</li></ul>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"See_Also\">See Also</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Nuclear_Fission&amp;action=edit&amp;section=12\" title=\"Edit section: See Also\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<ul><li><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Albert_Einstein\" title=\"Albert Einstein\">Albert Einstein</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Nuclear_Fusion\" title=\"Nuclear Fusion\">Nuclear Fusion</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Chain_Reaction&amp;action=edit&amp;redlink=1\" class=\"new\" title=\"Chain Reaction (page does not exist)\">Chain Reaction</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Radioactivity&amp;action=edit&amp;redlink=1\" class=\"new\" title=\"Radioactivity (page does not exist)\">Radioactivity</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Nuclear_Reactor&amp;action=edit&amp;redlink=1\" class=\"new\" title=\"Nuclear Reactor (page does not exist)\">Nuclear Reactor</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Critical_Mass&amp;action=edit&amp;redlink=1\" class=\"new\" title=\"Critical Mass (page does not exist)\">Critical Mass</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Einstein%27s_Mass%E2%80%93Energy_Equivalence&amp;action=edit&amp;redlink=1\" class=\"new\" title=\"Einstein&#39;s Mass–Energy Equivalence (page does not exist)\">Einstein's Mass–Energy Equivalence</a></li></ul>\n<!-- \nNewPP limit report\nCached time: 20261018101659\nCache expiry: 86400\nReduced expiry: false\nComplications: [show‐toc]\nCPU time usage: 0.027 seconds\nReal time usage: 0.039 seconds\nPreprocessor visited node count: 140/1000000\nPost‐expand include size: 0/2097152 bytes\nTemplate argument size: 0/2097152 bytes\nHighest expansion depth: 2/100\nExpensive parser function count: 0/100\nUnstrip recursion depth: 0/20\nUnstrip post‐expand size: 4158/5000000 bytes\n-->\n<!--\nTransclusion expansion time report (%,ms,calls,template)\n100.00%    0.000      1 -total\n-->\n\n<!-- Saved in parser cache with key wiki-wiki_:pcache:32674:|#|:idhash:canonical and timestamp 20261018101659 and revision id 251920. Rendering was triggered because: api-parse\n -->\n</div>"}